{"call_id":"call_qJBM2ij0fzSOnDcle4UycoxK","process_id":"24544","turn_id":"019ca636-11b3-7341-9df3-19da5388775f","command":["/bin/bash","-lc","cd /workspace && kspec tasks ready --eligible --json"],"cwd":"/workspace","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"kspec tasks ready --eligible --json"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","stdout":"[\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ17W53F5NN0CWTVSEY53R\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Ralph loop needs iteration-level timeout. When a spawned agent gets stuck (e.g., tool call hanging o\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"**What**: Ralph loop has no per-iteration timeout. When a spawned agent gets stuck (e.g., a tool call hanging on stdin, like the `kspec batch --commands \\\"\\\"` hang), ralph waits indefinitely. Claude Code's per-bash-command timeout (120s default) does not cover all hang scenarios — an agent can be alive but unresponsive at the ACP protocol level.\\n\\n**Where**:\\n- `src/cli/commands/ralph.ts` — main loop at line ~1127, the `agent.client.prompt()` call at line ~1373 and the reflection prompt call. These are the two main await points that can hang.\\n- `src/acp/client.ts` — the ACP client that manages the session/prompt lifecycle\\n\\n**How**:\\n- Add a configurable `--iteration-timeout <minutes>` CLI option (default: 20 minutes) to the ralph command registration (line ~868).\\n- Wrap the `agent.client.prompt()` calls in a `Promise.race` with a timeout. On timeout: (1) kill the stuck agent process via `agent.kill()`, (2) log the timeout as a failed iteration, (3) apply the existing retry/failure escalation logic.\\n- The timeout should cover the entire iteration (task-work prompt + optional reflection prompt), not individual prompts, since an iteration is the atomic unit of work.\\n- Consider using `AbortController` / `AbortSignal` if the ACP client supports it, otherwise use `Promise.race` with a timer.\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- `kspec ralph --iteration-timeout 15` kills a stuck agent after 15 minutes and continues to the next iteration (or exits if max failures reached)\\n- Default timeout is 20 minutes\\n- Timeout events are logged to the session event store with type `iteration.timeout`\\n- Existing retry and failure escalation logic handles timeouts the same as other iteration failures\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@cli-ralph\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:55.301Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQW7RZ6DDS9BNKW8XMDXF4\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:47:39.935Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"Linked to @cli-ralph spec. New feature: iteration-level timeout for ralph loop. Spec covers ralph command.\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/workspace/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1AF125H0FK29HE769R4P\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Ralph event handler iteration attribution should use session-scoped mapping instead of shared mutabl\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"**What**: Ralph event handler uses a `sessionIterationMap` (Map<string, number>) to attribute late ACP session updates to the correct iteration. However, the current implementation has a race condition: when a new ACP session starts for iteration N+1, late updates from iteration N's session can arrive after `sessionIterationMap.set(newSessionId, N+1)` but the old session's listener may still fire. The `_sid` closure variable in the `on('update')` handler captures the session ID correctly, but there is no cleanup of old listeners when starting a new session.\\n\\nThe Codex review on PR #484 identified that the shared mutable `sessionIterationMap` approach is fragile — a session-scoped listener pattern would be more robust.\\n\\n**Where**:\\n- `src/cli/commands/ralph.ts` line 1061 — `sessionIterationMap` declaration\\n- `src/cli/commands/ralph.ts` lines 1305-1337 — the `on('update')` handler that uses `sessionIterationMap.get(_sid)` for iteration lookup\\n- `src/cli/commands/ralph.ts` line 1369 — where new sessions are registered in the map\\n\\n**How**:\\nTwo approaches (pick one):\\n\\n1. **Session-scoped listeners** (recommended): Instead of one persistent `on('update')` handler, register a new listener per ACP session that captures the iteration number in its closure. Remove the old listener when the session ends or a new one starts. This eliminates the shared mutable map entirely.\\n\\n2. **Cleanup old sessions**: When starting a new ACP session, remove the previous session's entry from `sessionIterationMap` and call `agent.client.off('update', oldHandler)` to prevent stale callbacks. Less clean but simpler delta.\\n\\nBoth approaches should also ensure the translator is reset between iterations (it already creates a fresh translator per retry, but verify per-iteration reset).\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- Late updates from a previous ACP session are attributed to the correct iteration (the one that created that session)\\n- No shared mutable state for iteration tracking — either session-scoped closures or explicit cleanup\\n- Existing event logging tests still pass\\n- Event log entries show correct iteration numbers even when sessions overlap\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@cli-ralph\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:57.953Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQVH2H94SY215Y96TMWF0K\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:47:16.689Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"Linked to @cli-ralph spec. Ralph internal: session-scoped event listener mapping. Clear scope from enrichment.\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/workspace/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1B9C62DT55EK8WK01BP0\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Plan import warnings treated as errors in batch mode. The no YAML code block in specs section warnin\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"**What**: When `kspec plan import` runs inside batch mode, non-fatal warnings (e.g., 'no YAML code block in specs section') cause the batch to fail and roll back. The plan-import command uses `warn()` for non-fatal issues and `process.exit(EXIT_CODES.SUCCESS)` on completion. In batch mode, `process.exit()` is intercepted and throws `BatchExitError`. The exit code 0 is correctly detected as success (line 859 in batch-exec.ts). However, the warnings written to stderr via `warn()` are captured by `OutputCapture` and when the captured output is returned, it may be treated as an error indicator by the batch result processing.\\n\\nThe core issue is that `plan import` calls `process.exit(0)` even on success (line 687 of plan-import.ts). In batch mode, this throws `BatchExitError(0)`, which IS handled as success (batch-exec.ts line 859). But the warnings in captured stderr make the output look like a failure. Additionally, the batch atomic mode may see stderr output as part of the error string when `BatchExitError` code is non-zero for other plan import failures.\\n\\n**Where**:\\n- `src/cli/commands/plan-import.ts` line 687 — `process.exit(EXIT_CODES.SUCCESS)` which triggers BatchExitError in batch mode\\n- `src/cli/commands/plan-import.ts` lines 124-135 — warning emission for non-fatal parse errors\\n- `src/cli/batch-exec.ts` lines 849-875 — BatchExitError handling in `dispatchCommand()`\\n- `src/cli/batch-context.ts` — OutputCapture that captures both stdout and stderr\\n\\n**How**:\\n1. In `plan-import.ts`, replace the final `process.exit(EXIT_CODES.SUCCESS)` with a simple `return`. The batch executor expects normal returns for success — only use `process.exit()` for failures. This is the main fix.\\n2. Alternatively, ensure that `dispatchCommand()` in batch-exec.ts distinguishes between stderr warnings and actual errors when a `BatchExitError(0)` is caught — treat exit code 0 as success regardless of captured output content.\\n3. Consider adding a `warnings` field to `BatchCommandResult` so warnings can be reported without marking the command as failed.\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- `kspec plan import` with warnings runs successfully in batch mode (no rollback)\\n- Warnings are still visible in batch output (not silenced)\\n- Plan import still fails correctly in batch mode for actual errors (YAML parse errors, missing module, etc.)\\n- Add a test case: batch with a plan import that produces warnings should succeed\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@plan-import\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:58.796Z\",\n    \"notes\": [],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/workspace/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1C22TH9SEXXGBYQT8Y3Z\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Plan import --update replaces all ACs instead of merging. When extending an existing spec with a new\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"Plan import --update replaces all ACs instead of merging. When extending an existing spec with a new AC via plan import, --update overwrites acceptance_criteria entirely. Need AC-merge strategy: append new AC IDs, preserve existing ones. Encountered during spec-plan for session-log-show ac-10.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@plan-import\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:59.586Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ8ZD10EG4Z7MHEAHWBMAT\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:37:08.769Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"ENRICHMENT — Standalone context for this task:\\n\\n**What**: When `kspec plan import --update` encounters an existing spec, it replaces the entire `acceptance_criteria` array instead of merging. If a spec already has ac-1 through ac-9 and a plan adds ac-10, --update overwrites all ACs with only what the plan document contains, losing ac-1 through ac-9.\\n\\n**Where**: `src/cli/commands/plan-import.ts` lines 271-303 (the --update branch). The update logic builds a `Partial<SpecItemInput>` and sets `updates.acceptance_criteria = spec.acceptance_criteria` (line 296), passed to `updateSpecItem()` in `src/parser/yaml.ts` line 1522. `updateSpecItem` does direct property assignment (`targetObj[key] = value` at line 1560) with no merge logic.\\n\\n**How**: Implement an AC merge strategy in the update path. When `updates.acceptance_criteria` is set and the existing item already has ACs:\\n1. Index existing ACs by their `id` field (e.g., \\\"ac-1\\\", \\\"ac-2\\\")\\n2. For each AC in the plan update: if an AC with the same `id` exists, replace it; otherwise append it\\n3. Preserve existing ACs whose IDs are not in the update set\\nBest done in `plan-import.ts` before calling `updateSpecItem`, since general updateSpecItem callers may want full replacement semantics.\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- `kspec plan import --update` with a plan that adds one new AC to an existing spec preserves all pre-existing ACs and appends the new one\\n- A plan that modifies an existing AC (same id) updates that AC in place while preserving others\\n- Tests cover: append-only new AC, update existing AC by id, mixed append+update, no ACs in plan (preserves existing), empty existing ACs (creates all from plan)\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/workspace/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1CTV35WDA9G51NEH943K\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Agents should check plan_ref when starting tasks. If a task has plan_ref, run kspec plan get @plan-r\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"Agents should check plan_ref when starting tasks. If a task has plan_ref, run kspec plan get @plan-ref to read implementation notes, algorithm details, and context. Update task-work skill and task-work-loop workflow to include this step. Plans are the right place for rich implementation context instead of cramming it into task notes.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": null,\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:33:00.379Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ9E2V7MF4XRKW0MQQC59X\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:37:23.804Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"ENRICHMENT — Standalone context for this task:\\n\\n**What**: When agents start a task that has a `plan_ref` field, they should automatically retrieve the plan to get implementation notes, algorithm details, and design context. Currently the task-work skill and task-work-loop workflow do not mention checking plan_ref. Plans are the intended home for rich implementation context rather than cramming it into task descriptions or notes.\\n\\n**Where**: \\n- `templates/skills/task-work/SKILL.md` — Step 3 \\\"Start Task\\\" section (line 86-90) needs a substep: \\\"If task has plan_ref, run `kspec plan get @plan-ref` to read implementation notes\\\"\\n- Task-work-loop workflow definition in `.kspec/` (the workflow YAML) — add the same step\\n- `src/cli/commands/task.ts` — the `task get` output already shows `plan_ref` when present; the `task start` command could optionally print a hint like \\\"This task has a plan: run `kspec plan get @plan-ref` for implementation context\\\"\\n\\n**How**:\\n1. Update `templates/skills/task-work/SKILL.md` step 3 to add: \\\"If `kspec task get @ref` shows a `plan_ref`, run `kspec plan get @plan-ref` to read implementation notes and design context before starting work.\\\"\\n2. Update the task-work-loop workflow to include the same check in its automated flow\\n3. Optionally enhance `kspec task start` in `src/cli/commands/task.ts` to print an info message when the started task has a non-null plan_ref\\n4. Run `kspec agents generate` after template changes\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- The task-work skill documents checking plan_ref when starting tasks\\n- The task-work-loop workflow includes plan context retrieval\\n- Agents following the skill will automatically get plan context when a task has plan_ref\\n- `kspec agents generate` produces updated kspec-agents.md\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      },\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQVPYHQ56GVPJH0EKJ6TWV\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:47:22.705Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"Infra: Workflow/skill documentation change. Agents check plan_ref when starting tasks. No code change needed, skill template update.\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/workspace/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1DQMCBX32KDVWBJBF8W1\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"ULID short ref ambiguity in batch operations: when two ULIDs share a prefix (e.g. 01KJ4SNE9 and 01KJ\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"ULID short ref ambiguity in batch operations: when two ULIDs share a prefix (e.g. 01KJ4SNE9 and 01KJ4SNEC), short refs like @01KJ4SNE fail. kspec already has a shortest-unique-ID utility — this should be used anywhere refs are displayed (triage list, task list, inbox list, promote result_ref, etc.) so that presented refs are always unambiguous and copy-pasteable into subsequent commands.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@batch-exec\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:33:01.300Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQA0MV956KKZREMG980AE6\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:37:42.812Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"ENRICHMENT — Standalone context for this task:\\n\\n**What**: When two ULIDs share a prefix (e.g., 01KJ4SNE9... and 01KJ4SNEC...), the default 8-character short ref `@01KJ4SNE` is ambiguous and fails to resolve. The `ReferenceIndex.shortUlid()` method (in `src/parser/refs.ts` line 310) already computes the minimum unique prefix, but many places in the codebase use a hardcoded `_ulid.slice(0, 8)` instead. There are 69 occurrences of `_ulid.slice(0, 8)` across 19 files.\\n\\n**Where**: All places that format ULID refs for display need to use `shortUlid()` instead of `_ulid.slice(0, 8)`. Key files with hardcoded slicing:\\n- `src/cli/commands/triage.ts` (14 occurrences) — triage list, record, act, get, start\\n- `src/cli/commands/inbox.ts` (9 occurrences) — inbox list, add, promote\\n- `src/cli/commands/item.ts` (6 occurrences) — item list, get, add\\n- `src/cli/commands/plan.ts` (9 occurrences) — plan list, get\\n- `src/cli/commands/search.ts` (4 occurrences) — search results\\n- `src/cli/commands/session/context.ts` (4 occurrences) — session context display\\n- `src/triage/actions.ts` (1 occurrence) — promote result ref\\n- `src/parser/validate.ts` (8 occurrences) — validation output\\n- Several other files with 1-3 occurrences each\\n\\nThe `ReferenceIndex` is already available in most of these contexts (it's built during `initContext()` or `buildIndexes()`).\\n\\n**How**:\\n1. For each file, replace `_ulid.slice(0, 8)` with `index.shortUlid(_ulid)` where a ReferenceIndex is in scope\\n2. For files where no index is available, either thread the index through or create a lightweight shortening utility\\n3. For triage records (which aren't indexed in the main ReferenceIndex), consider building a local index or a dedicated triage ULID shortener\\n4. Ensure that refs displayed by any list/get/result command are always copy-pasteable into subsequent commands without ambiguity\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- No occurrences of `_ulid.slice(0, 8)` remain in display-facing code paths\\n- All displayed refs (triage list, task list, inbox list, search results, promote result_ref, etc.) use `shortUlid()` or equivalent\\n- A test with two items sharing an 8-character ULID prefix shows that displayed refs are still unique and resolvable\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/workspace/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1EKVV4V60V7WT6DCK3H3\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Triage promote action should optionally delete the inbox item after creating the task. Currently pro\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"Triage promote action should optionally delete the inbox item after creating the task. Currently promoted items stay in inbox requiring manual cleanup. Consider --consume flag or making deletion the default for promote (with snapshot preserved in triage record).\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@triage-cli-commands\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:33:02.203Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQAHM5SX18W9F5V0ET02G6\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:38:00.197Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"ENRICHMENT — Standalone context for this task:\\n\\n**What**: When `kspec triage act` executes a \\\"promote\\\" action, it creates a new task from the inbox item but leaves the original inbox item in the inbox. This requires a separate manual `kspec inbox delete` step to clean up, which is tedious and easy to forget. The inbox item's text is already preserved in the triage record's `item_snapshot` field, so the original is not needed for audit trail purposes.\\n\\n**Where**: \\n- `src/triage/actions.ts` lines 54-74 — the `promote` case in `executeTriageAction()`. Currently creates the task and returns, but never calls `deleteInboxItem()` for the promoted item.\\n- `src/cli/commands/triage.ts` lines 235-289 — the `triage act` command handler. This is where the `--consume` flag would be parsed and passed to `executeTriageAction()`.\\n- The `delete` and `duplicate` cases (lines 77-90, 117-130) already call `deleteInboxItem()` — the promote case can follow the same pattern.\\n\\n**How**: Two reasonable approaches:\\n1. **Flag approach**: Add `--consume` flag to `kspec triage act` that deletes the inbox item after promote. Pass it through `ExecuteTriageActionOptions` to `executeTriageAction()`.\\n2. **Default behavior**: Make deletion the default for promote (since the snapshot is preserved in the triage record), with a `--keep` flag to opt out.\\n\\nRecommendation: Option 2 (delete by default) is cleaner since every promoted item has its text preserved in `item_snapshot`. The `--keep` flag is the escape hatch. The `delete` and `duplicate` cases already delete inbox items as their default behavior, so promote doing the same is consistent.\\n\\nImplementation:\\n1. Add `consume` option to `ExecuteTriageActionOptions` (default true for promote)\\n2. In the `promote` case of `executeTriageAction()`, after creating the task, call `deleteInboxItem()` for `record.inbox_ref` unless consume is false\\n3. Add `--keep` flag to `kspec triage act` command that sets `consume: false`\\n4. Update dry-run output to mention deletion\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- `kspec triage act <ref>` on a promote record creates the task AND deletes the inbox item\\n- `kspec triage act <ref> --keep` creates the task but preserves the inbox item\\n- Dry-run output mentions that the inbox item would be deleted\\n- The `item_snapshot` in the triage record still contains the original text for audit trail\\n- Tests cover: promote with deletion (default), promote with --keep, dry-run output\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/workspace/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1FCWDDH5FM5JKSWNYVBX\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Web UI: persist all filter state and current view in URL query params so refreshing the page preserv\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"Web UI: persist all filter state and current view in URL query params so refreshing the page preserves state. Applies to all list/filter pages (triage, inbox, tasks, etc).\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@web-ui\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:33:03.004Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQB2J9SKA1QNC3SG3NN8WF\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:38:17.546Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"ENRICHMENT — Standalone context for this task:\\n\\n**What**: Web UI pages lose their filter state when the browser refreshes. For example, filtering tasks by status=in_progress or triage by tag=cli is lost on reload. Filter state should be persisted in URL query parameters so that refreshing preserves the view, and URLs are shareable/bookmarkable.\\n\\n**Where**: The tasks page (`packages/web-ui/src/routes/tasks/+page.svelte`) already partially implements URL-based filters — it reads from `$page.url.searchParams` (line 39-47) via the `filterParams` derived value. However, the triage page (`packages/web-ui/src/routes/triage/+page.svelte`) uses local `$state` variables (`filterTag`, `filterStatus` at lines 43-44) that are NOT synced to URL params. Other list pages (items, inbox) likely have the same issue.\\n\\nKey files to update:\\n- `packages/web-ui/src/routes/triage/+page.svelte` — filterTag and filterStatus need URL param sync\\n- `packages/web-ui/src/routes/items/+page.svelte` — check if filters use URL params\\n- Any other list/filter pages under `packages/web-ui/src/routes/`\\n\\n**How**: Follow the pattern already established in the tasks page:\\n1. Read initial filter values from `$page.url.searchParams` (from `$app/stores`)\\n2. When a filter changes, use `goto()` (from `$app/navigation`) to update URL params without a full page reload: `goto(\\\\`?status=\\\\${filterStatus}&tag=\\\\${filterTag}\\\\`, { replaceState: true, keepFocus: true })`\\n3. Use `$derived` to compute filter values from URL params (like tasks page does)\\n4. This approach gives: URL persistence, back/forward navigation, shareable links\\n\\nFor the triage page specifically:\\n- Replace `let filterTag = $state('')` with reading from `$page.url.searchParams.get('tag')`\\n- Replace `let filterStatus = $state('all')` with reading from `$page.url.searchParams.get('status')`\\n- Add `goto()` calls when filters change\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- All list/filter pages read initial state from URL query params\\n- Changing a filter updates the URL without full page reload\\n- Refreshing the page preserves filter state\\n- Browser back/forward navigates between filter states\\n- The triage page URL shows filters like `?status=untriaged&tag=cli`\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      },\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQWG9550XY4Y8F7V6W5W47\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:47:48.645Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"Linked to @web-ui module. URL state persistence for filter/view params across all list pages.\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/workspace/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1G6HBSJRXVSWEWQ9W9E4\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Web UI triage page: add filter controls for triage action types (promote, delete, defer, spec-gap, d\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"Web UI triage page: add filter controls for triage action types (promote, delete, defer, spec-gap, duplicate). Currently no way to filter triage records by action.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@interactive-triage-ui\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:33:03.825Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQBJ4QTDMCZJ4NAY7B1KGP\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:38:33.495Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"ENRICHMENT — Standalone context for this task:\\n\\n**What**: The triage page has status and tag filters (lines 326-347 of `packages/web-ui/src/routes/triage/+page.svelte`) but no way to filter triage records by their action type (promote, delete, defer, spec-gap, duplicate). When reviewing triage history, users often want to see \\\"all promote decisions\\\" or \\\"all spec-gap findings\\\" without scrolling through mixed results.\\n\\n**Where**: `packages/web-ui/src/routes/triage/+page.svelte`:\\n- Existing filters: `filterStatus` (all/untriaged/triaged/acted_on) at line 44, `filterTag` at line 43\\n- Filter UI: lines 324-347 (the filter controls div with data-testid=\\\"triage-filters\\\")\\n- Filtered items computation: `filteredItems` derived at lines 78-96 — currently filters by tag and status only\\n- The `TriageAction` type includes: promote, delete, defer, spec-gap, duplicate (defined in `$lib/types/triage`)\\n\\n**How**:\\n1. Add a new `filterAction` state variable: `let filterAction = $state<TriageAction | ''>('')`\\n2. Add a new `<select>` element in the filters div (alongside status and tag filters) with options for each action type\\n3. Add action filtering to the `filteredItems` derived computation:\\n   ```\\n   if (filterAction) {\\n     items = items.filter((i) => i.record?.action === filterAction);\\n   }\\n   ```\\n4. Include the action filter in the `$effect` that resets `currentIndex` when filters change (line 296-302)\\n5. If implementing URL state persistence (task @01KJDQ1F), also sync this filter to URL params\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- A dropdown/select control for triage action type appears in the filter bar\\n- Selecting \\\"promote\\\" shows only items with promote decisions\\n- Selecting \\\"spec-gap\\\" shows only spec-gap items\\n- The empty/default option shows all items (no action filter)\\n- Changing the filter resets the card navigation index to 0\\n- E2E test covers filtering by action type\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      },\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQWRKV7J9BSC3BY2NNPNG8\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:47:57.179Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"Linked to @interactive-triage-ui spec. Add filter controls for triage action types on triage page.\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/workspace/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1GZVK77Y8CETXW2E56TG\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Web UI WebSocket connection unreliable - continually disconnects and reconnects. Needs investigation\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"Web UI WebSocket connection unreliable - continually disconnects and reconnects. Needs investigation into reconnection logic, heartbeat handling, or fallback to polling.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@daemon-server\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:33:04.635Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQC6EMCTTQFKDBQX9JCG55\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:38:54.292Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"ENRICHMENT — Standalone context for this task:\\n\\n**What**: The Web UI WebSocket connection continually disconnects and reconnects. The `WebSocketManager` in `packages/web-ui/src/lib/websocket/manager.ts` has exponential backoff reconnection (1s, 2s, 4s... max 30s, max 10 attempts), but users report seeing frequent disconnect/reconnect cycles in the console logs. This suggests either the server is closing connections, the keepalive/heartbeat mechanism is missing, or reconnection resets are happening incorrectly.\\n\\n**Where**:\\n- `packages/web-ui/src/lib/websocket/manager.ts` — The WebSocketManager class. Key areas:\\n  - `connect()` (line 150): creates WebSocket, sets up onopen/onmessage/onerror/onclose handlers\\n  - `scheduleReconnect()` (line 371): exponential backoff logic, max 10 attempts\\n  - `onclose` handler (line 183): triggers reconnect on any close event, even clean ones\\n  - No heartbeat/ping mechanism — if the server expects periodic pings, the connection may time out\\n- `packages/web-ui/src/lib/stores/connection.svelte.ts` — Connection store wrapping the manager\\n- Server-side WebSocket handler (in the daemon code) — check if it sends pings or has idle timeouts\\n\\n**How**: Investigation and fix areas:\\n1. **Add heartbeat/ping**: Implement a periodic ping from client to server (e.g., every 30s). Many WebSocket servers close idle connections after a timeout (often 60s). The client should send a ping command and expect a pong response.\\n2. **Don't reconnect on clean close**: The `onclose` handler (line 183) calls `scheduleReconnect()` unconditionally. If `event.code === 1000` (normal closure) and the client initiated it via `disconnect()`, skip reconnection. Currently `disconnect()` clears the timer, but there's a race if onclose fires after disconnect resets state.\\n3. **Check server-side idle timeout**: The daemon's WebSocket server may have an idle timeout that closes connections. Either increase it or implement heartbeat to keep alive.\\n4. **Consider connection health check**: After reconnecting, verify the connection is actually healthy (e.g., send a ping and wait for response) before transitioning to 'connected' state.\\n5. **Investigate MAX_RECONNECT_ATTEMPTS**: After 10 failed reconnects, the connection is abandoned permanently. Consider resetting the attempt counter after a successful connection that lasts longer than some threshold (e.g., 60s).\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- WebSocket connection remains stable for extended periods without spurious disconnects\\n- Client sends periodic heartbeat/ping to prevent idle timeout\\n- Clean disconnects (code 1000 from client-initiated disconnect) don't trigger reconnection\\n- Console shows clean connection lifecycle without repeated disconnect/reconnect spam\\n- Connection lost indicator (10s threshold) only triggers for genuine server unavailability\\n- Tests cover: heartbeat keeps connection alive, clean close doesn't reconnect, reconnection works after genuine server restart\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      },\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQX0Y1T915X3M23D1VS9PP\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:48:05.698Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"Linked to @daemon-server spec (covers WebSocket protocol). Investigate and fix WebSocket reconnection reliability.\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/workspace/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  }\n]\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"[\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ17W53F5NN0CWTVSEY53R\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Ralph loop needs iteration-level timeout. When a spawned agent gets stuck (e.g., tool call hanging o\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"**What**: Ralph loop has no per-iteration timeout. When a spawned agent gets stuck (e.g., a tool call hanging on stdin, like the `kspec batch --commands \\\"\\\"` hang), ralph waits indefinitely. Claude Code's per-bash-command timeout (120s default) does not cover all hang scenarios — an agent can be alive but unresponsive at the ACP protocol level.\\n\\n**Where**:\\n- `src/cli/commands/ralph.ts` — main loop at line ~1127, the `agent.client.prompt()` call at line ~1373 and the reflection prompt call. These are the two main await points that can hang.\\n- `src/acp/client.ts` — the ACP client that manages the session/prompt lifecycle\\n\\n**How**:\\n- Add a configurable `--iteration-timeout <minutes>` CLI option (default: 20 minutes) to the ralph command registration (line ~868).\\n- Wrap the `agent.client.prompt()` calls in a `Promise.race` with a timeout. On timeout: (1) kill the stuck agent process via `agent.kill()`, (2) log the timeout as a failed iteration, (3) apply the existing retry/failure escalation logic.\\n- The timeout should cover the entire iteration (task-work prompt + optional reflection prompt), not individual prompts, since an iteration is the atomic unit of work.\\n- Consider using `AbortController` / `AbortSignal` if the ACP client supports it, otherwise use `Promise.race` with a timer.\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- `kspec ralph --iteration-timeout 15` kills a stuck agent after 15 minutes and continues to the next iteration (or exits if max failures reached)\\n- Default timeout is 20 minutes\\n- Timeout events are logged to the session event store with type `iteration.timeout`\\n- Existing retry and failure escalation logic handles timeouts the same as other iteration failures\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@cli-ralph\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:55.301Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQW7RZ6DDS9BNKW8XMDXF4\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:47:39.935Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"Linked to @cli-ralph spec. New feature: iteration-level timeout for ralph loop. Spec covers ralph command.\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/workspace/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1AF125H0FK29HE769R4P\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Ralph event handler iteration attribution should use session-scoped mapping instead of shared mutabl\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"**What**: Ralph event handler uses a `sessionIterationMap` (Map<string, number>) to attribute late ACP session updates to the correct iteration. However, the current implementation has a race condition: when a new ACP session starts for iteration N+1, late updates from iteration N's session can arrive after `sessionIterationMap.set(newSessionId, N+1)` but the old session's listener may still fire. The `_sid` closure variable in the `on('update')` handler captures the session ID correctly, but there is no cleanup of old listeners when starting a new session.\\n\\nThe Codex review on PR #484 identified that the shared mutable `sessionIterationMap` approach is fragile — a session-scoped listener pattern would be more robust.\\n\\n**Where**:\\n- `src/cli/commands/ralph.ts` line 1061 — `sessionIterationMap` declaration\\n- `src/cli/commands/ralph.ts` lines 1305-1337 — the `on('update')` handler that uses `sessionIterationMap.get(_sid)` for iteration lookup\\n- `src/cli/commands/ralph.ts` line 1369 — where new sessions are registered in the map\\n\\n**How**:\\nTwo approaches (pick one):\\n\\n1. **Session-scoped listeners** (recommended): Instead of one persistent `on('update')` handler, register a new listener per ACP session that captures the iteration number in its closure. Remove the old listener when the session ends or a new one starts. This eliminates the shared mutable map entirely.\\n\\n2. **Cleanup old sessions**: When starting a new ACP session, remove the previous session's entry from `sessionIterationMap` and call `agent.client.off('update', oldHandler)` to prevent stale callbacks. Less clean but simpler delta.\\n\\nBoth approaches should also ensure the translator is reset between iterations (it already creates a fresh translator per retry, but verify per-iteration reset).\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- Late updates from a previous ACP session are attributed to the correct iteration (the one that created that session)\\n- No shared mutable state for iteration tracking — either session-scoped closures or explicit cleanup\\n- Existing event logging tests still pass\\n- Event log entries show correct iteration numbers even when sessions overlap\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@cli-ralph\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:57.953Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQVH2H94SY215Y96TMWF0K\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:47:16.689Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"Linked to @cli-ralph spec. Ralph internal: session-scoped event listener mapping. Clear scope from enrichment.\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/workspace/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1B9C62DT55EK8WK01BP0\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Plan import warnings treated as errors in batch mode. The no YAML code block in specs section warnin\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"**What**: When `kspec plan import` runs inside batch mode, non-fatal warnings (e.g., 'no YAML code block in specs section') cause the batch to fail and roll back. The plan-import command uses `warn()` for non-fatal issues and `process.exit(EXIT_CODES.SUCCESS)` on completion. In batch mode, `process.exit()` is intercepted and throws `BatchExitError`. The exit code 0 is correctly detected as success (line 859 in batch-exec.ts). However, the warnings written to stderr via `warn()` are captured by `OutputCapture` and when the captured output is returned, it may be treated as an error indicator by the batch result processing.\\n\\nThe core issue is that `plan import` calls `process.exit(0)` even on success (line 687 of plan-import.ts). In batch mode, this throws `BatchExitError(0)`, which IS handled as success (batch-exec.ts line 859). But the warnings in captured stderr make the output look like a failure. Additionally, the batch atomic mode may see stderr output as part of the error string when `BatchExitError` code is non-zero for other plan import failures.\\n\\n**Where**:\\n- `src/cli/commands/plan-import.ts` line 687 — `process.exit(EXIT_CODES.SUCCESS)` which triggers BatchExitError in batch mode\\n- `src/cli/commands/plan-import.ts` lines 124-135 — warning emission for non-fatal parse errors\\n- `src/cli/batch-exec.ts` lines 849-875 — BatchExitError handling in `dispatchCommand()`\\n- `src/cli/batch-context.ts` — OutputCapture that captures both stdout and stderr\\n\\n**How**:\\n1. In `plan-import.ts`, replace the final `process.exit(EXIT_CODES.SUCCESS)` with a simple `return`. The batch executor expects normal returns for success — only use `process.exit()` for failures. This is the main fix.\\n2. Alternatively, ensure that `dispatchCommand()` in batch-exec.ts distinguishes between stderr warnings and actual errors when a `BatchExitError(0)` is caught — treat exit code 0 as success regardless of captured output content.\\n3. Consider adding a `warnings` field to `BatchCommandResult` so warnings can be reported without marking the command as failed.\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- `kspec plan import` with warnings runs successfully in batch mode (no rollback)\\n- Warnings are still visible in batch output (not silenced)\\n- Plan import still fails correctly in batch mode for actual errors (YAML parse errors, missing module, etc.)\\n- Add a test case: batch with a plan import that produces warnings should succeed\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@plan-import\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:58.796Z\",\n    \"notes\": [],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/workspace/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1C22TH9SEXXGBYQT8Y3Z\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Plan import --update replaces all ACs instead of merging. When extending an existing spec with a new\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"Plan import --update replaces all ACs instead of merging. When extending an existing spec with a new AC via plan import, --update overwrites acceptance_criteria entirely. Need AC-merge strategy: append new AC IDs, preserve existing ones. Encountered during spec-plan for session-log-show ac-10.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@plan-import\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:59.586Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ8ZD10EG4Z7MHEAHWBMAT\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:37:08.769Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"ENRICHMENT — Standalone context for this task:\\n\\n**What**: When `kspec plan import --update` encounters an existing spec, it replaces the entire `acceptance_criteria` array instead of merging. If a spec already has ac-1 through ac-9 and a plan adds ac-10, --update overwrites all ACs with only what the plan document contains, losing ac-1 through ac-9.\\n\\n**Where**: `src/cli/commands/plan-import.ts` lines 271-303 (the --update branch). The update logic builds a `Partial<SpecItemInput>` and sets `updates.acceptance_criteria = spec.acceptance_criteria` (line 296), passed to `updateSpecItem()` in `src/parser/yaml.ts` line 1522. `updateSpecItem` does direct property assignment (`targetObj[key] = value` at line 1560) with no merge logic.\\n\\n**How**: Implement an AC merge strategy in the update path. When `updates.acceptance_criteria` is set and the existing item already has ACs:\\n1. Index existing ACs by their `id` field (e.g., \\\"ac-1\\\", \\\"ac-2\\\")\\n2. For each AC in the plan update: if an AC with the same `id` exists, replace it; otherwise append it\\n3. Preserve existing ACs whose IDs are not in the update set\\nBest done in `plan-import.ts` before calling `updateSpecItem`, since general updateSpecItem callers may want full replacement semantics.\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- `kspec plan import --update` with a plan that adds one new AC to an existing spec preserves all pre-existing ACs and appends the new one\\n- A plan that modifies an existing AC (same id) updates that AC in place while preserving others\\n- Tests cover: append-only new AC, update existing AC by id, mixed append+update, no ACs in plan (preserves existing), empty existing ACs (creates all from plan)\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/workspace/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1CTV35WDA9G51NEH943K\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Agents should check plan_ref when starting tasks. If a task has plan_ref, run kspec plan get @plan-r\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"Agents should check plan_ref when starting tasks. If a task has plan_ref, run kspec plan get @plan-ref to read implementation notes, algorithm details, and context. Update task-work skill and task-work-loop workflow to include this step. Plans are the right place for rich implementation context instead of cramming it into task notes.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": null,\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:33:00.379Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ9E2V7MF4XRKW0MQQC59X\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:37:23.804Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"ENRICHMENT — Standalone context for this task:\\n\\n**What**: When agents start a task that has a `plan_ref` field, they should automatically retrieve the plan to get implementation notes, algorithm details, and design context. Currently the task-work skill and task-work-loop workflow do not mention checking plan_ref. Plans are the intended home for rich implementation context rather than cramming it into task descriptions or notes.\\n\\n**Where**: \\n- `templates/skills/task-work/SKILL.md` — Step 3 \\\"Start Task\\\" section (line 86-90) needs a substep: \\\"If task has plan_ref, run `kspec plan get @plan-ref` to read implementation notes\\\"\\n- Task-work-loop workflow definition in `.kspec/` (the workflow YAML) — add the same step\\n- `src/cli/commands/task.ts` — the `task get` output already shows `plan_ref` when present; the `task start` command could optionally print a hint like \\\"This task has a plan: run `kspec plan get @plan-ref` for implementation context\\\"\\n\\n**How**:\\n1. Update `templates/skills/task-work/SKILL.md` step 3 to add: \\\"If `kspec task get @ref` shows a `plan_ref`, run `kspec plan get @plan-ref` to read implementation notes and design context before starting work.\\\"\\n2. Update the task-work-loop workflow to include the same check in its automated flow\\n3. Optionally enhance `kspec task start` in `src/cli/commands/task.ts` to print an info message when the started task has a non-null plan_ref\\n4. Run `kspec agents generate` after template changes\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- The task-work skill documents checking plan_ref when starting tasks\\n- The task-work-loop workflow includes plan context retrieval\\n- Agents following the skill will automatically get plan context when a task has plan_ref\\n- `kspec agents generate` produces updated kspec-agents.md\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      },\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQVPYHQ56GVPJH0EKJ6TWV\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:47:22.705Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"Infra: Workflow/skill documentation change. Agents check plan_ref when starting tasks. No code change needed, skill template update.\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/workspace/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1DQMCBX32KDVWBJBF8W1\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"ULID short ref ambiguity in batch operations: when two ULIDs share a prefix (e.g. 01KJ4SNE9 and 01KJ\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"ULID short ref ambiguity in batch operations: when two ULIDs share a prefix (e.g. 01KJ4SNE9 and 01KJ4SNEC), short refs like @01KJ4SNE fail. kspec already has a shortest-unique-ID utility — this should be used anywhere refs are displayed (triage list, task list, inbox list, promote result_ref, etc.) so that presented refs are always unambiguous and copy-pasteable into subsequent commands.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@batch-exec\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:33:01.300Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQA0MV956KKZREMG980AE6\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:37:42.812Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"ENRICHMENT — Standalone context for this task:\\n\\n**What**: When two ULIDs share a prefix (e.g., 01KJ4SNE9... and 01KJ4SNEC...), the default 8-character short ref `@01KJ4SNE` is ambiguous and fails to resolve. The `ReferenceIndex.shortUlid()` method (in `src/parser/refs.ts` line 310) already computes the minimum unique prefix, but many places in the codebase use a hardcoded `_ulid.slice(0, 8)` instead. There are 69 occurrences of `_ulid.slice(0, 8)` across 19 files.\\n\\n**Where**: All places that format ULID refs for display need to use `shortUlid()` instead of `_ulid.slice(0, 8)`. Key files with hardcoded slicing:\\n- `src/cli/commands/triage.ts` (14 occurrences) — triage list, record, act, get, start\\n- `src/cli/commands/inbox.ts` (9 occurrences) — inbox list, add, promote\\n- `src/cli/commands/item.ts` (6 occurrences) — item list, get, add\\n- `src/cli/commands/plan.ts` (9 occurrences) — plan list, get\\n- `src/cli/commands/search.ts` (4 occurrences) — search results\\n- `src/cli/commands/session/context.ts` (4 occurrences) — session context display\\n- `src/triage/actions.ts` (1 occurrence) — promote result ref\\n- `src/parser/validate.ts` (8 occurrences) — validation output\\n- Several other files with 1-3 occurrences each\\n\\nThe `ReferenceIndex` is already available in most of these contexts (it's built during `initContext()` or `buildIndexes()`).\\n\\n**How**:\\n1. For each file, replace `_ulid.slice(0, 8)` with `index.shortUlid(_ulid)` where a ReferenceIndex is in scope\\n2. For files where no index is available, either thread the index through or create a lightweight shortening utility\\n3. For triage records (which aren't indexed in the main ReferenceIndex), consider building a local index or a dedicated triage ULID shortener\\n4. Ensure that refs displayed by any list/get/result command are always copy-pasteable into subsequent commands without ambiguity\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- No occurrences of `_ulid.slice(0, 8)` remain in display-facing code paths\\n- All displayed refs (triage list, task list, inbox list, search results, promote result_ref, etc.) use `shortUlid()` or equivalent\\n- A test with two items sharing an 8-character ULID prefix shows that displayed refs are still unique and resolvable\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/workspace/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1EKVV4V60V7WT6DCK3H3\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Triage promote action should optionally delete the inbox item after creating the task. Currently pro\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"Triage promote action should optionally delete the inbox item after creating the task. Currently promoted items stay in inbox requiring manual cleanup. Consider --consume flag or making deletion the default for promote (with snapshot preserved in triage record).\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@triage-cli-commands\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:33:02.203Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQAHM5SX18W9F5V0ET02G6\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:38:00.197Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"ENRICHMENT — Standalone context for this task:\\n\\n**What**: When `kspec triage act` executes a \\\"promote\\\" action, it creates a new task from the inbox item but leaves the original inbox item in the inbox. This requires a separate manual `kspec inbox delete` step to clean up, which is tedious and easy to forget. The inbox item's text is already preserved in the triage record's `item_snapshot` field, so the original is not needed for audit trail purposes.\\n\\n**Where**: \\n- `src/triage/actions.ts` lines 54-74 — the `promote` case in `executeTriageAction()`. Currently creates the task and returns, but never calls `deleteInboxItem()` for the promoted item.\\n- `src/cli/commands/triage.ts` lines 235-289 — the `triage act` command handler. This is where the `--consume` flag would be parsed and passed to `executeTriageAction()`.\\n- The `delete` and `duplicate` cases (lines 77-90, 117-130) already call `deleteInboxItem()` — the promote case can follow the same pattern.\\n\\n**How**: Two reasonable approaches:\\n1. **Flag approach**: Add `--consume` flag to `kspec triage act` that deletes the inbox item after promote. Pass it through `ExecuteTriageActionOptions` to `executeTriageAction()`.\\n2. **Default behavior**: Make deletion the default for promote (since the snapshot is preserved in the triage record), with a `--keep` flag to opt out.\\n\\nRecommendation: Option 2 (delete by default) is cleaner since every promoted item has its text preserved in `item_snapshot`. The `--keep` flag is the escape hatch. The `delete` and `duplicate` cases already delete inbox items as their default behavior, so promote doing the same is consistent.\\n\\nImplementation:\\n1. Add `consume` option to `ExecuteTriageActionOptions` (default true for promote)\\n2. In the `promote` case of `executeTriageAction()`, after creating the task, call `deleteInboxItem()` for `record.inbox_ref` unless consume is false\\n3. Add `--keep` flag to `kspec triage act` command that sets `consume: false`\\n4. Update dry-run output to mention deletion\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- `kspec triage act <ref>` on a promote record creates the task AND deletes the inbox item\\n- `kspec triage act <ref> --keep` creates the task but preserves the inbox item\\n- Dry-run output mentions that the inbox item would be deleted\\n- The `item_snapshot` in the triage record still contains the original text for audit trail\\n- Tests cover: promote with deletion (default), promote with --keep, dry-run output\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/workspace/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1FCWDDH5FM5JKSWNYVBX\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Web UI: persist all filter state and current view in URL query params so refreshing the page preserv\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"Web UI: persist all filter state and current view in URL query params so refreshing the page preserves state. Applies to all list/filter pages (triage, inbox, tasks, etc).\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@web-ui\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:33:03.004Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQB2J9SKA1QNC3SG3NN8WF\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:38:17.546Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"ENRICHMENT — Standalone context for this task:\\n\\n**What**: Web UI pages lose their filter state when the browser refreshes. For example, filtering tasks by status=in_progress or triage by tag=cli is lost on reload. Filter state should be persisted in URL query parameters so that refreshing preserves the view, and URLs are shareable/bookmarkable.\\n\\n**Where**: The tasks page (`packages/web-ui/src/routes/tasks/+page.svelte`) already partially implements URL-based filters — it reads from `$page.url.searchParams` (line 39-47) via the `filterParams` derived value. However, the triage page (`packages/web-ui/src/routes/triage/+page.svelte`) uses local `$state` variables (`filterTag`, `filterStatus` at lines 43-44) that are NOT synced to URL params. Other list pages (items, inbox) likely have the same issue.\\n\\nKey files to update:\\n- `packages/web-ui/src/routes/triage/+page.svelte` — filterTag and filterStatus need URL param sync\\n- `packages/web-ui/src/routes/items/+page.svelte` — check if filters use URL params\\n- Any other list/filter pages under `packages/web-ui/src/routes/`\\n\\n**How**: Follow the pattern already established in the tasks page:\\n1. Read initial filter values from `$page.url.searchParams` (from `$app/stores`)\\n2. When a filter changes, use `goto()` (from `$app/navigation`) to update URL params without a full page reload: `goto(\\\\`?status=\\\\${filterStatus}&tag=\\\\${filterTag}\\\\`, { replaceState: true, keepFocus: true })`\\n3. Use `$derived` to compute filter values from URL params (like tasks page does)\\n4. This approach gives: URL persistence, back/forward navigation, shareable links\\n\\nFor the triage page specifically:\\n- Replace `let filterTag = $state('')` with reading from `$page.url.searchParams.get('tag')`\\n- Replace `let filterStatus = $state('all')` with reading from `$page.url.searchParams.get('status')`\\n- Add `goto()` calls when filters change\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- All list/filter pages read initial state from URL query params\\n- Changing a filter updates the URL without full page reload\\n- Refreshing the page preserves filter state\\n- Browser back/forward navigates between filter states\\n- The triage page URL shows filters like `?status=untriaged&tag=cli`\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      },\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQWG9550XY4Y8F7V6W5W47\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:47:48.645Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"Linked to @web-ui module. URL state persistence for filter/view params across all list pages.\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/workspace/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1G6HBSJRXVSWEWQ9W9E4\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Web UI triage page: add filter controls for triage action types (promote, delete, defer, spec-gap, d\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"Web UI triage page: add filter controls for triage action types (promote, delete, defer, spec-gap, duplicate). Currently no way to filter triage records by action.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@interactive-triage-ui\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:33:03.825Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQBJ4QTDMCZJ4NAY7B1KGP\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:38:33.495Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"ENRICHMENT — Standalone context for this task:\\n\\n**What**: The triage page has status and tag filters (lines 326-347 of `packages/web-ui/src/routes/triage/+page.svelte`) but no way to filter triage records by their action type (promote, delete, defer, spec-gap, duplicate). When reviewing triage history, users often want to see \\\"all promote decisions\\\" or \\\"all spec-gap findings\\\" without scrolling through mixed results.\\n\\n**Where**: `packages/web-ui/src/routes/triage/+page.svelte`:\\n- Existing filters: `filterStatus` (all/untriaged/triaged/acted_on) at line 44, `filterTag` at line 43\\n- Filter UI: lines 324-347 (the filter controls div with data-testid=\\\"triage-filters\\\")\\n- Filtered items computation: `filteredItems` derived at lines 78-96 — currently filters by tag and status only\\n- The `TriageAction` type includes: promote, delete, defer, spec-gap, duplicate (defined in `$lib/types/triage`)\\n\\n**How**:\\n1. Add a new `filterAction` state variable: `let filterAction = $state<TriageAction | ''>('')`\\n2. Add a new `<select>` element in the filters div (alongside status and tag filters) with options for each action type\\n3. Add action filtering to the `filteredItems` derived computation:\\n   ```\\n   if (filterAction) {\\n     items = items.filter((i) => i.record?.action === filterAction);\\n   }\\n   ```\\n4. Include the action filter in the `$effect` that resets `currentIndex` when filters change (line 296-302)\\n5. If implementing URL state persistence (task @01KJDQ1F), also sync this filter to URL params\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- A dropdown/select control for triage action type appears in the filter bar\\n- Selecting \\\"promote\\\" shows only items with promote decisions\\n- Selecting \\\"spec-gap\\\" shows only spec-gap items\\n- The empty/default option shows all items (no action filter)\\n- Changing the filter resets the card navigation index to 0\\n- E2E test covers filtering by action type\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      },\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQWRKV7J9BSC3BY2NNPNG8\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:47:57.179Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"Linked to @interactive-triage-ui spec. Add filter controls for triage action types on triage page.\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/workspace/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1GZVK77Y8CETXW2E56TG\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Web UI WebSocket connection unreliable - continually disconnects and reconnects. Needs investigation\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"Web UI WebSocket connection unreliable - continually disconnects and reconnects. Needs investigation into reconnection logic, heartbeat handling, or fallback to polling.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@daemon-server\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:33:04.635Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQC6EMCTTQFKDBQX9JCG55\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:38:54.292Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"ENRICHMENT — Standalone context for this task:\\n\\n**What**: The Web UI WebSocket connection continually disconnects and reconnects. The `WebSocketManager` in `packages/web-ui/src/lib/websocket/manager.ts` has exponential backoff reconnection (1s, 2s, 4s... max 30s, max 10 attempts), but users report seeing frequent disconnect/reconnect cycles in the console logs. This suggests either the server is closing connections, the keepalive/heartbeat mechanism is missing, or reconnection resets are happening incorrectly.\\n\\n**Where**:\\n- `packages/web-ui/src/lib/websocket/manager.ts` — The WebSocketManager class. Key areas:\\n  - `connect()` (line 150): creates WebSocket, sets up onopen/onmessage/onerror/onclose handlers\\n  - `scheduleReconnect()` (line 371): exponential backoff logic, max 10 attempts\\n  - `onclose` handler (line 183): triggers reconnect on any close event, even clean ones\\n  - No heartbeat/ping mechanism — if the server expects periodic pings, the connection may time out\\n- `packages/web-ui/src/lib/stores/connection.svelte.ts` — Connection store wrapping the manager\\n- Server-side WebSocket handler (in the daemon code) — check if it sends pings or has idle timeouts\\n\\n**How**: Investigation and fix areas:\\n1. **Add heartbeat/ping**: Implement a periodic ping from client to server (e.g., every 30s). Many WebSocket servers close idle connections after a timeout (often 60s). The client should send a ping command and expect a pong response.\\n2. **Don't reconnect on clean close**: The `onclose` handler (line 183) calls `scheduleReconnect()` unconditionally. If `event.code === 1000` (normal closure) and the client initiated it via `disconnect()`, skip reconnection. Currently `disconnect()` clears the timer, but there's a race if onclose fires after disconnect resets state.\\n3. **Check server-side idle timeout**: The daemon's WebSocket server may have an idle timeout that closes connections. Either increase it or implement heartbeat to keep alive.\\n4. **Consider connection health check**: After reconnecting, verify the connection is actually healthy (e.g., send a ping and wait for response) before transitioning to 'connected' state.\\n5. **Investigate MAX_RECONNECT_ATTEMPTS**: After 10 failed reconnects, the connection is abandoned permanently. Consider resetting the attempt counter after a successful connection that lasts longer than some threshold (e.g., 60s).\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- WebSocket connection remains stable for extended periods without spurious disconnects\\n- Client sends periodic heartbeat/ping to prevent idle timeout\\n- Clean disconnects (code 1000 from client-initiated disconnect) don't trigger reconnection\\n- Console shows clean connection lifecycle without repeated disconnect/reconnect spam\\n- Connection lost indicator (10s threshold) only triggers for genuine server unavailability\\n- Tests cover: heartbeat keeps connection alive, clean close doesn't reconnect, reconnection works after genuine server restart\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      },\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQX0Y1T915X3M23D1VS9PP\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:48:05.698Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"Linked to @daemon-server spec (covers WebSocket protocol). Investigate and fix WebSocket reconnection reliability.\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/workspace/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  }\n]\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":2,"nanos":427183211},"formatted_output":"[\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ17W53F5NN0CWTVSEY53R\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Ralph loop needs iteration-level timeout. When a spawned agent gets stuck (e.g., tool call hanging o\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"**What**: Ralph loop has no per-iteration timeout. When a spawned agent gets stuck (e.g., a tool call hanging on stdin, like the `kspec batch --commands \\\"\\\"` hang), ralph waits indefinitely. Claude Code's per-bash-command timeout (120s default) does not cover all hang scenarios — an agent can be alive but unresponsive at the ACP protocol level.\\n\\n**Where**:\\n- `src/cli/commands/ralph.ts` — main loop at line ~1127, the `agent.client.prompt()` call at line ~1373 and the reflection prompt call. These are the two main await points that can hang.\\n- `src/acp/client.ts` — the ACP client that manages the session/prompt lifecycle\\n\\n**How**:\\n- Add a configurable `--iteration-timeout <minutes>` CLI option (default: 20 minutes) to the ralph command registration (line ~868).\\n- Wrap the `agent.client.prompt()` calls in a `Promise.race` with a timeout. On timeout: (1) kill the stuck agent process via `agent.kill()`, (2) log the timeout as a failed iteration, (3) apply the existing retry/failure escalation logic.\\n- The timeout should cover the entire iteration (task-work prompt + optional reflection prompt), not individual prompts, since an iteration is the atomic unit of work.\\n- Consider using `AbortController` / `AbortSignal` if the ACP client supports it, otherwise use `Promise.race` with a timer.\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- `kspec ralph --iteration-timeout 15` kills a stuck agent after 15 minutes and continues to the next iteration (or exits if max failures reached)\\n- Default timeout is 20 minutes\\n- Timeout events are logged to the session event store with type `iteration.timeout`\\n- Existing retry and failure escalation logic handles timeouts the same as other iteration failures\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@cli-ralph\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:55.301Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQW7RZ6DDS9BNKW8XMDXF4\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:47:39.935Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"Linked to @cli-ralph spec. New feature: iteration-level timeout for ralph loop. Spec covers ralph command.\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/workspace/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1AF125H0FK29HE769R4P\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Ralph event handler iteration attribution should use session-scoped mapping instead of shared mutabl\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"**What**: Ralph event handler uses a `sessionIterationMap` (Map<string, number>) to attribute late ACP session updates to the correct iteration. However, the current implementation has a race condition: when a new ACP session starts for iteration N+1, late updates from iteration N's session can arrive after `sessionIterationMap.set(newSessionId, N+1)` but the old session's listener may still fire. The `_sid` closure variable in the `on('update')` handler captures the session ID correctly, but there is no cleanup of old listeners when starting a new session.\\n\\nThe Codex review on PR #484 identified that the shared mutable `sessionIterationMap` approach is fragile — a session-scoped listener pattern would be more robust.\\n\\n**Where**:\\n- `src/cli/commands/ralph.ts` line 1061 — `sessionIterationMap` declaration\\n- `src/cli/commands/ralph.ts` lines 1305-1337 — the `on('update')` handler that uses `sessionIterationMap.get(_sid)` for iteration lookup\\n- `src/cli/commands/ralph.ts` line 1369 — where new sessions are registered in the map\\n\\n**How**:\\nTwo approaches (pick one):\\n\\n1. **Session-scoped listeners** (recommended): Instead of one persistent `on('update')` handler, register a new listener per ACP session that captures the iteration number in its closure. Remove the old listener when the session ends or a new one starts. This eliminates the shared mutable map entirely.\\n\\n2. **Cleanup old sessions**: When starting a new ACP session, remove the previous session's entry from `sessionIterationMap` and call `agent.client.off('update', oldHandler)` to prevent stale callbacks. Less clean but simpler delta.\\n\\nBoth approaches should also ensure the translator is reset between iterations (it already creates a fresh translator per retry, but verify per-iteration reset).\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- Late updates from a previous ACP session are attributed to the correct iteration (the one that created that session)\\n- No shared mutable state for iteration tracking — either session-scoped closures or explicit cleanup\\n- Existing event logging tests still pass\\n- Event log entries show correct iteration numbers even when sessions overlap\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@cli-ralph\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:57.953Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQVH2H94SY215Y96TMWF0K\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:47:16.689Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"Linked to @cli-ralph spec. Ralph internal: session-scoped event listener mapping. Clear scope from enrichment.\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/workspace/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1B9C62DT55EK8WK01BP0\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Plan import warnings treated as errors in batch mode. The no YAML code block in specs section warnin\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"**What**: When `kspec plan import` runs inside batch mode, non-fatal warnings (e.g., 'no YAML code block in specs section') cause the batch to fail and roll back. The plan-import command uses `warn()` for non-fatal issues and `process.exit(EXIT_CODES.SUCCESS)` on completion. In batch mode, `process.exit()` is intercepted and throws `BatchExitError`. The exit code 0 is correctly detected as success (line 859 in batch-exec.ts). However, the warnings written to stderr via `warn()` are captured by `OutputCapture` and when the captured output is returned, it may be treated as an error indicator by the batch result processing.\\n\\nThe core issue is that `plan import` calls `process.exit(0)` even on success (line 687 of plan-import.ts). In batch mode, this throws `BatchExitError(0)`, which IS handled as success (batch-exec.ts line 859). But the warnings in captured stderr make the output look like a failure. Additionally, the batch atomic mode may see stderr output as part of the error string when `BatchExitError` code is non-zero for other plan import failures.\\n\\n**Where**:\\n- `src/cli/commands/plan-import.ts` line 687 — `process.exit(EXIT_CODES.SUCCESS)` which triggers BatchExitError in batch mode\\n- `src/cli/commands/plan-import.ts` lines 124-135 — warning emission for non-fatal parse errors\\n- `src/cli/batch-exec.ts` lines 849-875 — BatchExitError handling in `dispatchCommand()`\\n- `src/cli/batch-context.ts` — OutputCapture that captures both stdout and stderr\\n\\n**How**:\\n1. In `plan-import.ts`, replace the final `process.exit(EXIT_CODES.SUCCESS)` with a simple `return`. The batch executor expects normal returns for success — only use `process.exit()` for failures. This is the main fix.\\n2. Alternatively, ensure that `dispatchCommand()` in batch-exec.ts distinguishes between stderr warnings and actual errors when a `BatchExitError(0)` is caught — treat exit code 0 as success regardless of captured output content.\\n3. Consider adding a `warnings` field to `BatchCommandResult` so warnings can be reported without marking the command as failed.\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- `kspec plan import` with warnings runs successfully in batch mode (no rollback)\\n- Warnings are still visible in batch output (not silenced)\\n- Plan import still fails correctly in batch mode for actual errors (YAML parse errors, missing module, etc.)\\n- Add a test case: batch with a plan import that produces warnings should succeed\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@plan-import\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:58.796Z\",\n    \"notes\": [],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/workspace/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1C22TH9SEXXGBYQT8Y3Z\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Plan import --update replaces all ACs instead of merging. When extending an existing spec with a new\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"Plan import --update replaces all ACs instead of merging. When extending an existing spec with a new AC via plan import, --update overwrites acceptance_criteria entirely. Need AC-merge strategy: append new AC IDs, preserve existing ones. Encountered during spec-plan for session-log-show ac-10.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@plan-import\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:59.586Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ8ZD10EG4Z7MHEAHWBMAT\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:37:08.769Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"ENRICHMENT — Standalone context for this task:\\n\\n**What**: When `kspec plan import --update` encounters an existing spec, it replaces the entire `acceptance_criteria` array instead of merging. If a spec already has ac-1 through ac-9 and a plan adds ac-10, --update overwrites all ACs with only what the plan document contains, losing ac-1 through ac-9.\\n\\n**Where**: `src/cli/commands/plan-import.ts` lines 271-303 (the --update branch). The update logic builds a `Partial<SpecItemInput>` and sets `updates.acceptance_criteria = spec.acceptance_criteria` (line 296), passed to `updateSpecItem()` in `src/parser/yaml.ts` line 1522. `updateSpecItem` does direct property assignment (`targetObj[key] = value` at line 1560) with no merge logic.\\n\\n**How**: Implement an AC merge strategy in the update path. When `updates.acceptance_criteria` is set and the existing item already has ACs:\\n1. Index existing ACs by their `id` field (e.g., \\\"ac-1\\\", \\\"ac-2\\\")\\n2. For each AC in the plan update: if an AC with the same `id` exists, replace it; otherwise append it\\n3. Preserve existing ACs whose IDs are not in the update set\\nBest done in `plan-import.ts` before calling `updateSpecItem`, since general updateSpecItem callers may want full replacement semantics.\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- `kspec plan import --update` with a plan that adds one new AC to an existing spec preserves all pre-existing ACs and appends the new one\\n- A plan that modifies an existing AC (same id) updates that AC in place while preserving others\\n- Tests cover: append-only new AC, update existing AC by id, mixed append+update, no ACs in plan (preserves existing), empty existing ACs (creates all from plan)\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/workspace/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1CTV35WDA9G51NEH943K\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Agents should check plan_ref when starting tasks. If a task has plan_ref, run kspec plan get @plan-r\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"Agents should check plan_ref when starting tasks. If a task has plan_ref, run kspec plan get @plan-ref to read implementation notes, algorithm details, and context. Update task-work skill and task-work-loop workflow to include this step. Plans are the right place for rich implementation context instead of cramming it into task notes.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": null,\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:33:00.379Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ9E2V7MF4XRKW0MQQC59X\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:37:23.804Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"ENRICHMENT — Standalone context for this task:\\n\\n**What**: When agents start a task that has a `plan_ref` field, they should automatically retrieve the plan to get implementation notes, algorithm details, and design context. Currently the task-work skill and task-work-loop workflow do not mention checking plan_ref. Plans are the intended home for rich implementation context rather than cramming it into task descriptions or notes.\\n\\n**Where**: \\n- `templates/skills/task-work/SKILL.md` — Step 3 \\\"Start Task\\\" section (line 86-90) needs a substep: \\\"If task has plan_ref, run `kspec plan get @plan-ref` to read implementation notes\\\"\\n- Task-work-loop workflow definition in `.kspec/` (the workflow YAML) — add the same step\\n- `src/cli/commands/task.ts` — the `task get` output already shows `plan_ref` when present; the `task start` command could optionally print a hint like \\\"This task has a plan: run `kspec plan get @plan-ref` for implementation context\\\"\\n\\n**How**:\\n1. Update `templates/skills/task-work/SKILL.md` step 3 to add: \\\"If `kspec task get @ref` shows a `plan_ref`, run `kspec plan get @plan-ref` to read implementation notes and design context before starting work.\\\"\\n2. Update the task-work-loop workflow to include the same check in its automated flow\\n3. Optionally enhance `kspec task start` in `src/cli/commands/task.ts` to print an info message when the started task has a non-null plan_ref\\n4. Run `kspec agents generate` after template changes\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- The task-work skill documents checking plan_ref when starting tasks\\n- The task-work-loop workflow includes plan context retrieval\\n- Agents following the skill will automatically get plan context when a task has plan_ref\\n- `kspec agents generate` produces updated kspec-agents.md\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      },\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQVPYHQ56GVPJH0EKJ6TWV\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:47:22.705Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"Infra: Workflow/skill documentation change. Agents check plan_ref when starting tasks. No code change needed, skill template update.\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/workspace/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1DQMCBX32KDVWBJBF8W1\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"ULID short ref ambiguity in batch operations: when two ULIDs share a prefix (e.g. 01KJ4SNE9 and 01KJ\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"ULID short ref ambiguity in batch operations: when two ULIDs share a prefix (e.g. 01KJ4SNE9 and 01KJ4SNEC), short refs like @01KJ4SNE fail. kspec already has a shortest-unique-ID utility — this should be used anywhere refs are displayed (triage list, task list, inbox list, promote result_ref, etc.) so that presented refs are always unambiguous and copy-pasteable into subsequent commands.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@batch-exec\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:33:01.300Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQA0MV956KKZREMG980AE6\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:37:42.812Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"ENRICHMENT — Standalone context for this task:\\n\\n**What**: When two ULIDs share a prefix (e.g., 01KJ4SNE9... and 01KJ4SNEC...), the default 8-character short ref `@01KJ4SNE` is ambiguous and fails to resolve. The `ReferenceIndex.shortUlid()` method (in `src/parser/refs.ts` line 310) already computes the minimum unique prefix, but many places in the codebase use a hardcoded `_ulid.slice(0, 8)` instead. There are 69 occurrences of `_ulid.slice(0, 8)` across 19 files.\\n\\n**Where**: All places that format ULID refs for display need to use `shortUlid()` instead of `_ulid.slice(0, 8)`. Key files with hardcoded slicing:\\n- `src/cli/commands/triage.ts` (14 occurrences) — triage list, record, act, get, start\\n- `src/cli/commands/inbox.ts` (9 occurrences) — inbox list, add, promote\\n- `src/cli/commands/item.ts` (6 occurrences) — item list, get, add\\n- `src/cli/commands/plan.ts` (9 occurrences) — plan list, get\\n- `src/cli/commands/search.ts` (4 occurrences) — search results\\n- `src/cli/commands/session/context.ts` (4 occurrences) — session context display\\n- `src/triage/actions.ts` (1 occurrence) — promote result ref\\n- `src/parser/validate.ts` (8 occurrences) — validation output\\n- Several other files with 1-3 occurrences each\\n\\nThe `ReferenceIndex` is already available in most of these contexts (it's built during `initContext()` or `buildIndexes()`).\\n\\n**How**:\\n1. For each file, replace `_ulid.slice(0, 8)` with `index.shortUlid(_ulid)` where a ReferenceIndex is in scope\\n2. For files where no index is available, either thread the index through or create a lightweight shortening utility\\n3. For triage records (which aren't indexed in the main ReferenceIndex), consider building a local index or a dedicated triage ULID shortener\\n4. Ensure that refs displayed by any list/get/result command are always copy-pasteable into subsequent commands without ambiguity\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- No occurrences of `_ulid.slice(0, 8)` remain in display-facing code paths\\n- All displayed refs (triage list, task list, inbox list, search results, promote result_ref, etc.) use `shortUlid()` or equivalent\\n- A test with two items sharing an 8-character ULID prefix shows that displayed refs are still unique and resolvable\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/workspace/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1EKVV4V60V7WT6DCK3H3\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Triage promote action should optionally delete the inbox item after creating the task. Currently pro\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"Triage promote action should optionally delete the inbox item after creating the task. Currently promoted items stay in inbox requiring manual cleanup. Consider --consume flag or making deletion the default for promote (with snapshot preserved in triage record).\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@triage-cli-commands\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:33:02.203Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQAHM5SX18W9F5V0ET02G6\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:38:00.197Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"ENRICHMENT — Standalone context for this task:\\n\\n**What**: When `kspec triage act` executes a \\\"promote\\\" action, it creates a new task from the inbox item but leaves the original inbox item in the inbox. This requires a separate manual `kspec inbox delete` step to clean up, which is tedious and easy to forget. The inbox item's text is already preserved in the triage record's `item_snapshot` field, so the original is not needed for audit trail purposes.\\n\\n**Where**: \\n- `src/triage/actions.ts` lines 54-74 — the `promote` case in `executeTriageAction()`. Currently creates the task and returns, but never calls `deleteInboxItem()` for the promoted item.\\n- `src/cli/commands/triage.ts` lines 235-289 — the `triage act` command handler. This is where the `--consume` flag would be parsed and passed to `executeTriageAction()`.\\n- The `delete` and `duplicate` cases (lines 77-90, 117-130) already call `deleteInboxItem()` — the promote case can follow the same pattern.\\n\\n**How**: Two reasonable approaches:\\n1. **Flag approach**: Add `--consume` flag to `kspec triage act` that deletes the inbox item after promote. Pass it through `ExecuteTriageActionOptions` to `executeTriageAction()`.\\n2. **Default behavior**: Make deletion the default for promote (since the snapshot is preserved in the triage record), with a `--keep` flag to opt out.\\n\\nRecommendation: Option 2 (delete by default) is cleaner since every promoted item has its text preserved in `item_snapshot`. The `--keep` flag is the escape hatch. The `delete` and `duplicate` cases already delete inbox items as their default behavior, so promote doing the same is consistent.\\n\\nImplementation:\\n1. Add `consume` option to `ExecuteTriageActionOptions` (default true for promote)\\n2. In the `promote` case of `executeTriageAction()`, after creating the task, call `deleteInboxItem()` for `record.inbox_ref` unless consume is false\\n3. Add `--keep` flag to `kspec triage act` command that sets `consume: false`\\n4. Update dry-run output to mention deletion\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- `kspec triage act <ref>` on a promote record creates the task AND deletes the inbox item\\n- `kspec triage act <ref> --keep` creates the task but preserves the inbox item\\n- Dry-run output mentions that the inbox item would be deleted\\n- The `item_snapshot` in the triage record still contains the original text for audit trail\\n- Tests cover: promote with deletion (default), promote with --keep, dry-run output\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/workspace/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1FCWDDH5FM5JKSWNYVBX\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Web UI: persist all filter state and current view in URL query params so refreshing the page preserv\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"Web UI: persist all filter state and current view in URL query params so refreshing the page preserves state. Applies to all list/filter pages (triage, inbox, tasks, etc).\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@web-ui\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:33:03.004Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQB2J9SKA1QNC3SG3NN8WF\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:38:17.546Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"ENRICHMENT — Standalone context for this task:\\n\\n**What**: Web UI pages lose their filter state when the browser refreshes. For example, filtering tasks by status=in_progress or triage by tag=cli is lost on reload. Filter state should be persisted in URL query parameters so that refreshing preserves the view, and URLs are shareable/bookmarkable.\\n\\n**Where**: The tasks page (`packages/web-ui/src/routes/tasks/+page.svelte`) already partially implements URL-based filters — it reads from `$page.url.searchParams` (line 39-47) via the `filterParams` derived value. However, the triage page (`packages/web-ui/src/routes/triage/+page.svelte`) uses local `$state` variables (`filterTag`, `filterStatus` at lines 43-44) that are NOT synced to URL params. Other list pages (items, inbox) likely have the same issue.\\n\\nKey files to update:\\n- `packages/web-ui/src/routes/triage/+page.svelte` — filterTag and filterStatus need URL param sync\\n- `packages/web-ui/src/routes/items/+page.svelte` — check if filters use URL params\\n- Any other list/filter pages under `packages/web-ui/src/routes/`\\n\\n**How**: Follow the pattern already established in the tasks page:\\n1. Read initial filter values from `$page.url.searchParams` (from `$app/stores`)\\n2. When a filter changes, use `goto()` (from `$app/navigation`) to update URL params without a full page reload: `goto(\\\\`?status=\\\\${filterStatus}&tag=\\\\${filterTag}\\\\`, { replaceState: true, keepFocus: true })`\\n3. Use `$derived` to compute filter values from URL params (like tasks page does)\\n4. This approach gives: URL persistence, back/forward navigation, shareable links\\n\\nFor the triage page specifically:\\n- Replace `let filterTag = $state('')` with reading from `$page.url.searchParams.get('tag')`\\n- Replace `let filterStatus = $state('all')` with reading from `$page.url.searchParams.get('status')`\\n- Add `goto()` calls when filters change\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- All list/filter pages read initial state from URL query params\\n- Changing a filter updates the URL without full page reload\\n- Refreshing the page preserves filter state\\n- Browser back/forward navigates between filter states\\n- The triage page URL shows filters like `?status=untriaged&tag=cli`\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      },\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQWG9550XY4Y8F7V6W5W47\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:47:48.645Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"Linked to @web-ui module. URL state persistence for filter/view params across all list pages.\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/workspace/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1G6HBSJRXVSWEWQ9W9E4\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Web UI triage page: add filter controls for triage action types (promote, delete, defer, spec-gap, d\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"Web UI triage page: add filter controls for triage action types (promote, delete, defer, spec-gap, duplicate). Currently no way to filter triage records by action.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@interactive-triage-ui\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:33:03.825Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQBJ4QTDMCZJ4NAY7B1KGP\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:38:33.495Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"ENRICHMENT — Standalone context for this task:\\n\\n**What**: The triage page has status and tag filters (lines 326-347 of `packages/web-ui/src/routes/triage/+page.svelte`) but no way to filter triage records by their action type (promote, delete, defer, spec-gap, duplicate). When reviewing triage history, users often want to see \\\"all promote decisions\\\" or \\\"all spec-gap findings\\\" without scrolling through mixed results.\\n\\n**Where**: `packages/web-ui/src/routes/triage/+page.svelte`:\\n- Existing filters: `filterStatus` (all/untriaged/triaged/acted_on) at line 44, `filterTag` at line 43\\n- Filter UI: lines 324-347 (the filter controls div with data-testid=\\\"triage-filters\\\")\\n- Filtered items computation: `filteredItems` derived at lines 78-96 — currently filters by tag and status only\\n- The `TriageAction` type includes: promote, delete, defer, spec-gap, duplicate (defined in `$lib/types/triage`)\\n\\n**How**:\\n1. Add a new `filterAction` state variable: `let filterAction = $state<TriageAction | ''>('')`\\n2. Add a new `<select>` element in the filters div (alongside status and tag filters) with options for each action type\\n3. Add action filtering to the `filteredItems` derived computation:\\n   ```\\n   if (filterAction) {\\n     items = items.filter((i) => i.record?.action === filterAction);\\n   }\\n   ```\\n4. Include the action filter in the `$effect` that resets `currentIndex` when filters change (line 296-302)\\n5. If implementing URL state persistence (task @01KJDQ1F), also sync this filter to URL params\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- A dropdown/select control for triage action type appears in the filter bar\\n- Selecting \\\"promote\\\" shows only items with promote decisions\\n- Selecting \\\"spec-gap\\\" shows only spec-gap items\\n- The empty/default option shows all items (no action filter)\\n- Changing the filter resets the card navigation index to 0\\n- E2E test covers filtering by action type\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      },\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQWRKV7J9BSC3BY2NNPNG8\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:47:57.179Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"Linked to @interactive-triage-ui spec. Add filter controls for triage action types on triage page.\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/workspace/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1GZVK77Y8CETXW2E56TG\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Web UI WebSocket connection unreliable - continually disconnects and reconnects. Needs investigation\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"Web UI WebSocket connection unreliable - continually disconnects and reconnects. Needs investigation into reconnection logic, heartbeat handling, or fallback to polling.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@daemon-server\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:33:04.635Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQC6EMCTTQFKDBQX9JCG55\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:38:54.292Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"ENRICHMENT — Standalone context for this task:\\n\\n**What**: The Web UI WebSocket connection continually disconnects and reconnects. The `WebSocketManager` in `packages/web-ui/src/lib/websocket/manager.ts` has exponential backoff reconnection (1s, 2s, 4s... max 30s, max 10 attempts), but users report seeing frequent disconnect/reconnect cycles in the console logs. This suggests either the server is closing connections, the keepalive/heartbeat mechanism is missing, or reconnection resets are happening incorrectly.\\n\\n**Where**:\\n- `packages/web-ui/src/lib/websocket/manager.ts` — The WebSocketManager class. Key areas:\\n  - `connect()` (line 150): creates WebSocket, sets up onopen/onmessage/onerror/onclose handlers\\n  - `scheduleReconnect()` (line 371): exponential backoff logic, max 10 attempts\\n  - `onclose` handler (line 183): triggers reconnect on any close event, even clean ones\\n  - No heartbeat/ping mechanism — if the server expects periodic pings, the connection may time out\\n- `packages/web-ui/src/lib/stores/connection.svelte.ts` — Connection store wrapping the manager\\n- Server-side WebSocket handler (in the daemon code) — check if it sends pings or has idle timeouts\\n\\n**How**: Investigation and fix areas:\\n1. **Add heartbeat/ping**: Implement a periodic ping from client to server (e.g., every 30s). Many WebSocket servers close idle connections after a timeout (often 60s). The client should send a ping command and expect a pong response.\\n2. **Don't reconnect on clean close**: The `onclose` handler (line 183) calls `scheduleReconnect()` unconditionally. If `event.code === 1000` (normal closure) and the client initiated it via `disconnect()`, skip reconnection. Currently `disconnect()` clears the timer, but there's a race if onclose fires after disconnect resets state.\\n3. **Check server-side idle timeout**: The daemon's WebSocket server may have an idle timeout that closes connections. Either increase it or implement heartbeat to keep alive.\\n4. **Consider connection health check**: After reconnecting, verify the connection is actually healthy (e.g., send a ping and wait for response) before transitioning to 'connected' state.\\n5. **Investigate MAX_RECONNECT_ATTEMPTS**: After 10 failed reconnects, the connection is abandoned permanently. Consider resetting the attempt counter after a successful connection that lasts longer than some threshold (e.g., 60s).\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- WebSocket connection remains stable for extended periods without spurious disconnects\\n- Client sends periodic heartbeat/ping to prevent idle timeout\\n- Clean disconnects (code 1000 from client-initiated disconnect) don't trigger reconnection\\n- Console shows clean connection lifecycle without repeated disconnect/reconnect spam\\n- Connection lost indicator (10s threshold) only triggers for genuine server unavailability\\n- Tests cover: heartbeat keeps connection alive, clean close doesn't reconnect, reconnection works after genuine server restart\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      },\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQX0Y1T915X3M23D1VS9PP\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:48:05.698Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"Linked to @daemon-server spec (covers WebSocket protocol). Investigate and fix WebSocket reconnection reliability.\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/workspace/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  }\n]\n","status":"completed"}